{{main|1950 FIFA World Cup final round}}
The road to the title in the 1950 World Cup was unique; instead of a knockout stage, the preliminary group stage was followed by another [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] group. Of the 16 teams slated to compete, only 13 arrived.<ref name=":0" /> The final four teams were [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] (the host country and joint-top scorers from the group stage, coming from wins over [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] and [[Yugoslavia national football team|Yugoslavia]] and a draw against [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]]), [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] (who only had to play one match in their group, an 8–0 win over [[Bolivia national football team|Bolivia]]), [[Spain national football team|Spain]] (who won all three of their group matches, against [[England national football team|England]], [[Chile national football team|Chile]], and the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]]), and [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]] (who qualified ahead of [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] and the defending world champions, [[Italy national football team|Italy]]).

Brazil won both of their first two matches convincingly, beating Sweden 7–1 and Spain 6–1 to go top of the group with four points going into the final match. With three points, Uruguay were close behind in second place, although they had to come back from 2–1 down to draw 2–2 with Spain and beat Sweden 3–2, the winning goal coming just five minutes before the end of the game. The match between Brazil and Uruguay, on the other hand, would decide the title; a victory or a draw would grant Brazil the title, whereas Uruguay had to win the match in order to win the championship. Brazil had scored 21 goals in five games before the match with Uruguay and had defeated both Spain and Sweden with larger margins than the Uruguayans had. As a result, Brazil was extremely confident of victory in the deciding match, and newspapers and politicians declared victory before the game even began.<ref name=":0" /> 

The [[1950 FIFA World Cup]] was the only version of the tournament to be played with a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] final round; as such, is the only FIFA World Cup to date to not have a [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|deciding knock-out final]]. However, as the result of the match directly determined the champions, the match has come to be commonly referred to as the final, even by FIFA itself.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |url=http://www.brazilmax.com/news4.cfm/tborigem/fe_sports/id/4 |title=Fateful Final: 1950 World Cup in Rio de Janeiro's Maracanã |first=Alex |last=Bellos |date=29 March 2004 |work=Futebol: The Brazilian Way of Life |publisher=brazilmax.com |access-date=20 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100918091744/http://www.brazilmax.com/news4.cfm/tborigem/fe_sports/id/4 |archive-date=18 September 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=FIFA World Cup Finals since 1930|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-301_09a_wc-finals_alltime_8864.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071129182236/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-301_09a_wc-finals_alltime_8864.pdf |archive-date=29 November 2007 |publisher=FIFA |access-date=31 January 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Anticipated celebration==
The specialised press and the general public were so confident of victory, based on Brazil's almost indomitable form, that they had already started declaring Brazil the new world champions for days prior to the match. Newspapers such as the ''Gazeta Esportiva'' in [[São Paulo]] and ''O Mundo'' in Rio de Janeiro proclaimed victory the day before the game.<ref name=":0" /> Brazil had won their last two matches (Spain and Sweden) with a very successful attack-minded style of play. Uruguay, however, had encountered difficulties, managing only a draw against Spain and narrow victory over Sweden. The comparison of those results seemed to show that the Brazilians were set to defeat Uruguay as easily as they had defeated Spain and Sweden.

Moreover, in the [[Copa América]], also [[1949 South American Championship|held in Brazil the previous year]], the hosts had won by scoring 46 goals in just eight matches. Ecuador was beaten 9–1, Bolivia 10–1, and runners-up Paraguay were defeated with a margin of 7–0. Further, Brazil beat Uruguay 5–1.

Twenty-two gold medals were made with each player's name imprinted on them{{Citation needed|date=June 2018}} and the mayor of Rio, {{ill|Ângelo Mendes de Moraes|pt}}, delivered a speech on the day of the game with the words: "You, players, who in less than a few hours will be hailed as champions by millions of compatriots! You, who have no rivals in the entire hemisphere! You, who will overcome any other competitor! You, who I already salute as victors!"<ref name=":0" /> A victory song, "Brasil Os Vencedores" ("Brazil the Victors"), was composed and practised, ready to be played after the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=The Maracanã Blow: Brazil's Stunning World Cup Defeat |url=https://www.history.com/articles/the-maracana-blow-brazils-stunning-world-cup-defeat |website=History.com |access-date=23 October 2025 |archive-date=27 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250927110005/https://www.history.com/articles/the-maracana-blow-brazils-stunning-world-cup-defeat |url-status=live }}</ref>

However, Paulo Machado de Carvalho, then a [[São Paulo FC]] leader, but later head of the Brazilian squad that won the World Cups of [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]] and [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], opposed such premature claims of victory. During a visit to the training session at the [[Estádio São Januário]] on the eve of the game, Paulo found several politicians making impassioned speeches to the players, as well as journalists, photographers, and others arriving to join the "future champions". When he warned coach [[Flávio Costa]] about the risk of upsetting the players' concentration, Paulo was ignored. Frustrated, he told his son Tuta, who was with him, "we are going to lose".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Cardoso |first1=Tom |last2=Rockmann |first2=Roberto |title=O Marechal da Vitória: Uma História de Rádio, TV e Futebol |year=2005 |trans-title=The Marshall of Victory: A History of Radio, TV and Football |language=pt |location=[[São Paulo]] |publisher=A Girafa |publication-date=2005 |page=144 |isbn=9788589876759 }}</ref>

On the morning of 16 July 1950, the streets of [[Rio de Janeiro]] were bustling with activity. An improvised carnival was organised, with thousands of signs celebrating the world title, and chants of "Brazil must win!". This spirit was maintained until the final minutes of the match. The officially recorded crowd of 173,850 people at the [[Maracanã Stadium]] is an all-time high in terms of number of paid spectators for a football game; the actual attendance, including the thousands who entered the stadium illegally, amounted to up to 220,000 by some estimates,<ref name="attendance">{{cite web|url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2018/6/world-cup-rewind-world-cup-rewind-largest-attendance-at-a-match-in-the-1950-brazil-final|title=World Cup Rewind: Largest attendance at a match in the 1950 Brazil final|date=12 June 2018|first=Mike|last=Janela|publisher=[[Guinness World Records]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240204073738/http://guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2018/6/world-cup-rewind-world-cup-rewind-largest-attendance-at-a-match-in-the-1950-brazil-final|archive-date=4 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|author1=Sambafoot |title=Sambafoot.com: Maracanã, the largest stadium of the world |work=Sambafoot |date=28 November 2005 |url=http://www.sambafoot.com/en/news/19321_maracana__the_largest_stadium_of_the_world.html |access-date=20 June 2014 |publisher=sambafoot.com |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141115004043/http://www.sambafoot.com/en/news/19321_maracana__the_largest_stadium_of_the_world.html |archive-date=15 November 2014 |url-status=live }}</ref> and may only have been surpassed by that of the [[1923 FA Cup final]] in England, which anywhere between 150,000 and over 300,000 people are believed to have attended. Those figures are unlikely to be approached in an era when practically all high-profile matches are held in [[all-seater stadium]]s; until its first great remodelling in 1999, the Maracanã was mostly concrete grandstands with no seats.

==Uruguay's preparation==
The Brazilian newspaper ''O Mundo'' printed an early edition on the day of the final containing a photograph of Brazil with the caption "These are the world champions". Disgusted with the premature assumption, Uruguay's captain, [[Obdulio Varela]], bought as many copies as he could, laid them on his bathroom floor and encouraged his teammates to [[Spitting|spit]] and [[urinate]] on them.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wilson |first=Jonathan |url=https://www.si.com/more-sports/2010/07/04/uruguay-history |work=CNN |title=Uruguay's 1950 World Cup triumph a testament to the spirit of garra |date=4 July 2010 |access-date=27 November 2022 |archive-date=27 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221127232529/https://www.si.com/more-sports/2010/07/04/uruguay-history |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the moments prior to the match, coach [[Juan López (football manager, born 1908)|Juan López]] informed his team in Uruguay's dressing room that their best chance of surviving the powerful offensive line of Brazil would come through adopting a defensive strategy. After he left, Varela stood up and addressed the team himself, saying "Juancito is a good man, but today, he is wrong. If we play defensively against Brazil, our fate will be no different from Spain or Sweden."<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Hughes |first=Rob |date=11 June 2014 |title=In Brazil, It's Time for the World to Play |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/sports/soccer/in-brazil-its-time-for-the-world-to-play.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160721072256/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/12/sports/soccer/in-brazil-its-time-for-the-world-to-play.html |archive-date=21 July 2016 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]]}} {{subscription required}}</ref> Varela then delivered an emotional speech about how they should go against all odds and not be intimidated by the fans or the opposing team. The speech, as was later confirmed, played a huge part in the outcome of the game. In response to his squad's underdog status, the captain delivered the memorable line, "{{lang|es|Muchachos, los de afuera son de palo. Que comience la función.}}" ("Boys, outsiders are just stickdolls. Let's start the show." or "Outsiders don't play. Let the show begin.")<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Gill |first=A.A. |date=2010-06-10 |title=Playing for the World |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/culture/2010/06/world-cup-article-201006 |access-date=2024-01-14 |magazine=Vanity Fair |language=en-US |archive-date=10 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220810112524/https://www.vanityfair.com/culture/2010/06/world-cup-article-201006 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Gol ghiggia vs brasil.jpg|thumb|[[Alcides Ghiggia]] celebrates after scoring the 2nd goal.]]
The game began as predicted, with Brazil attacking against the Uruguayan defensive line for the majority of the first half. Unlike Spain and Sweden, however, the Uruguayans managed to maintain their defence and the first half ended scoreless. Brazil scored the first goal of the match only two minutes after the interval, with [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] forward Friaça shooting low past goalkeeper [[Roque Máspoli]]. After the goal, Varela took the ball and disputed the validity of the goal to the referee, arguing that Friaça was [[Offside (association football)|offside]]. Varela drew out this argument, going so far as to demand that the referee listen to him through an interpreter.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://sports.ndtv.com/fifa-world-cup-2014/news/225467-in-brazil-it-s-time-for-the-world-to-play|title=In Brazil, It's Time For the World to Play|first=Rob|last=Hughes|newspaper=The New York Times|date=12 June 2014|access-date=20 June 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140615182450/http://sports.ndtv.com/fifa-world-cup-2014/news/225467-in-brazil-it-s-time-for-the-world-to-play|archive-date=15 June 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> By the time the conversation ended, the crowd had calmed down. Then, Varela took the ball to the center of the field, and shouted to his team, "Now, it's time to win!"{{citation needed|date=April 2014}}

Uruguay managed to take control of the game. When faced with a capable Uruguayan attack, Brazil showed their frail defense, and [[Juan Alberto Schiaffino]] scored the equaliser in the 66th minute. Later, [[Alcides Ghiggia]], running down the right side of the field, scored another goal, with a low shot that went just under goalkeeper [[Moacir Barbosa]] (who, having anticipated a cross from Ghiggia's position, dived a moment too late to stop the ball from rolling under him), with only 11 minutes remaining on the clock. The crowd went virtually silent after the second Uruguayan goal until English referee [[George Reader]] signalled the end of the match, with the final score being 2–1 to Uruguay.

