When the match ended, the stadium was filled with "disturbing and traumatic absolute silence,"<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Galeano |first1=Eduardo |last2=Nogueira |first2=Armando |date=30 June 2009 |title=Silêncio no Maracanã |url=http://revistadehistoria.com.br/secao/artigos-revista/silencio-no-maracana |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131204061417/http://revistadehistoria.com.br/secao/artigos-revista/silencio-no-maracana |archive-date=4 December 2013 |website=Revista de História |language=pt-BR}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |date=30 April 2013 |title=Jogos Eternos – Brasil 1x2 Uruguai 1950 |url=https://imortaisdofutebol.com/jogos-eternos-brasil-1x2-uruguai-1950/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131026080948/http://imortaisdofutebol.com/2013/04/30/jogos-eternos-brasil-1x2-uruguai-1950/ |archive-date=26 October 2013 |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=Imortais do Futebol |language=pt-BR}}</ref> and famous radio journalist [[Ary Barroso]] (briefly) retired after the match.{{Citation needed|date=January 2024|reason=Needs a contemporary source, preferably in Portuguese or Spanish.}} A group of Brazilian fans started a fight with Uruguayans in a hotel lobby,<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 July 1950 |title=Incidente desagradavel entre torcedores brasileiros e turistas uruguaios |url=https://memoria.bn.gov.br/DocReader/DocReader.aspx?bib=116408&pagfis=49406 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=A Manhã |location=Rio de Janeiro |pages=14 |language=pt-BR}}</ref> and eight Uruguayans died as a result of celebrations in Uruguay.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 July 1950 |title=A EMOÇÃO FOI FATAL PARA OITO URUGUAIOS |url=https://memoria.bn.gov.br/DocReader/docreader.aspx?bib=116408&pasta=ano%20195&pesq=&pagfis=49403 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=A Manhã |location=Rio de Janeiro |pages=11 |language=pt-BR |archive-date=2 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240902083751/https://memoria.bn.gov.br/DocReader/docreader.aspx?bib=116408&pasta=ano%20195&pesq=&pagfis=49403 |url-status=live }}</ref> However, the players and most Brazilian fans behaved well in defeat; the Uruguayan newspaper ''El Dia'' declared Brazil "the other winner" and said "if before the match we respected the Brazilian sporting power, after the encounter our respect grew and we even more profoundly admired the great spirit demonstrated by the Brazilians in adversity."<ref>{{Cite news |date=23 July 1950 |title="Obdulio Varela para Presidente da República" |url=https://hemeroteca2.cultura.sc.gov.br/docreader/DocReader.aspx?bib=886440&pagfis=7290 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=Correio do Povo |location=Santa Catarina |pages=4 |language=pt-BR |archive-date=14 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240114214118/https://hemeroteca2.cultura.sc.gov.br/docreader/DocReader.aspx?bib=886440&pagfis=7290 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

Four members of the 1950 team—the captain [[Augusto da Costa|Augusto]], [[Juvenal (footballer, born November 1923)|Juvenal]], [[Bigode]] and [[Chico (footballer, born 1922)|Chico]]—never played for Brazil again. Two unused squad members of the 1950 team, [[Nílton Santos]] and [[Carlos Castilho]], were also members of the victorious Brazil squads that were to come, when Brazil won back-to-back World Cups in [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]] and [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]]. Santos played in both finals whereas Castilho only played in the 1954 FIFA World Cup and in 2007 was posthumously awarded the 1958 and 1962 winning medals as a squad member, having been [[Gilmar]]'s reserve in both tournaments. 

